ABSTRACT: Online advertisements (ads) play a critical role in sustaining the mobile ecosystem. However, imperfect ad review mechanisms allow ads to be exploited as a channel for malicious content dissemination, posing significant security risks to users and platforms. Existing malicious advertisement detection methods heavily rely on manually crafted rules, which limit automation and undermine detection robustness in complex and dynamic environments. To address these limitations, this paper proposes AdSeeker, an automated malicious ad detection framework driven by an autonomous agent. AdSeeker leverages the agent's decision-making capability to systematically explore application interfaces and collect multi-dimensional ad-related evidence, including ad SDK metadata, permission usage, network redirection links, and ad image elements. Based on the collected evidence, AdSeeker classifies malicious ad behaviors, assesses risk levels, and provides remediation suggestions, enabling quantitative security evaluation of mobile applications. We evaluate AdSeeker on 30,000 applications from AndroZoo. The results show that 10,497 apps contain ads, from which 14,971 ad samples are collected, including 1,537 malicious ads. AdSeeker achieves a detection precision of 96.30%. Our findings further reveal that malicious ads are present even in legitimate applications, highlighting the necessity for strengthened ad network auditing and more effective malicious ad governance to foster a safer mobile ecosystem. The source code is publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/AnaY1115/AdSeeker. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0118] year=2026 | venue=ISADES 2026 - 2nd International Symposium on AI-Driven Engineering Systems, Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Mapping the Research Landscape of Large Language Models in Network Intrusion Detection
KEYWORDS: Anomaly Detection; BERT; Bibliometric Analysis; Cyber Security; Generative AI; Large Language Models (LLM); Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS); Transformer; Anomaly detection; Artificial intelligence; Computational linguistics; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Growth rate; Mapping; Natural language processing systems; Network intrusion; Network security; Anomaly detection; BERT; Bibliometrics analysis; Cyber security; Generative AI; Language model; Large language model; Network intrusion detection system; Network intrusion detection systems; Transformer; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) remain a critical component of modern cybersecurity infrastructure; however, conventional signature- and rule-based approaches are increasingly challenged by novel, polymorphic, and rapidly evolving attack patterns. In this context, large language models (LLMs), transformer architectures, and generative AI approaches have attracted increasing attention as potential tools for analysing network traffic, security logs, and cyber threat patterns. This study presents a PRISMA 2020-guided bibliometric analysis of LLM- and transformer-based NIDS research published between 2010 and 2025. A comprehensive Boolean search strategy was applied to the Web of Science Core Collection and Scopus databases, yielding 11,381 records. After DOI- and title-based duplicate removal, document type, year, language screening, and dual-condition topic-eligibility assessment, a final analytical corpus of 814 peer-reviewed journal articles was retained. The corpus was analysed using the R bibliometrix package and VOSviewer through performance analysis, citation and co-citation analysis, Bradford's Law, Lotka's Law, keyword co-occurrence, reference publication year spectroscopy and thematic mapping. The findings show that research activity remained marginal until 2020, with only 11 articles before 2021, but expanded sharply after the emergence and diffusion of transformer-based security applications, reaching 578 articles in 2025, representing 71.0% of the corpus. The long-term compound annual growth rate was 69.9% for 2013-2025, whereas the post-inflection growth rate reached 213.3% for 2021-2025. China was the leading contributor, with 374 articles (45.9%), followed by India and the United States, while IEEE Access was the most productive source, with 102 articles (12.5%). Cocitation and keyword analyses indicate that Transformer- and BERT-related foundations constitute the intellectual core of the field, whereas XAI, federated learning, and autonomous security agents remain underrepresented but strategically important research directions. Overall, this study provides an evidence-based map of the publication dynamics, intellectual structure, and emerging priorities of network intrusion detection research based on LLMs and transformers. © 2026 IEEE.

