ABSTRACT: The rapid proliferation of Artificial Intelligence Generated Content (AIGC) has shifted the information ecology from a 'Search Paradigm' to a 'Generative Paradigm,' challenging traditional university information literacy education. This paper examines the limitations of traditional, instrumentally rational models in addressing emerging risks like 'algorithmic hallucinations' and cognitive inertia. Highlighting learners' shift toward a dialogue-oriented model, the study emphasizes integrating prompt engineering and algorithmic literacy as core competencies. To adapt, we propose a pedagogical transition toward intelligent governance, establishing a 'three-dimensional collaborative ecosystem' (educators, learners, AI agents). We recommend upgrading the Blended Flipped Classroom via a 'pre-fetching, gaming, and auditing' closed-loop and adopting the cybersecurity 'Red Teaming' mindset to build adversarial learning environments. Finally, future research must prioritize dynamic process auditing, dynamic adversarial feature repositories, and interdisciplinary general education systems. © 2026 IEEE.

