ABSTRACT: Penetration testing remains a critical component of modern cybersecurity, enabling the proactive identification of system vulnerabilities before exploitation by adversaries. However, traditional penetration testing is highly dependent on expert knowledge, manual coordination of tools, and time-intensive analysis, limiting its scalability and consistency. This study proposes a hybrid multi-agent penetration testing framework based on Large Language Models (LLMs) to enhance automation and decision-making in offensive security workflows. The research includes a systematic analysis of recent advances in LLM-driven cybersecurity, multi-agent architectures, reinforcement learning integration, and vulnerability assessment techniques. A conceptual architecture is developed, incorporating specialized agents, structured memory, tool-oriented reasoning, and a validation layer with human-in-the-loop supervision. The proposed system is experimentally evaluated in a controlled environment using standard penetration testing tools and an LLM interface. The results demonstrate improved efficiency in reconnaissance analysis, vulnerability interpretation, and report generation, with observed reductions in analysis time. However, limitations related to contextual consistency, reliability, and adversarial robustness persist. The study contributes a structured and safety-aware framework for LLM-assisted penetration testing and outlines key challenges and future directions for autonomous cybersecurity systems. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0122] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Intelligent Multimedia, Networking, and Security, IMNS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Whispers of Wealth: A Systematic Red-Teaming Study of the Agent Payments Protocol (AP2)
KEYWORDS: Agent Payments Protocol (AP2); agent security; AI red teaming; LLM agents; prompt injection; Artificial intelligence; Cryptography; Electronic money; Intelligent agents; Network security; Agent payment protocol (AP2); Agent security; AI red teaming; Language model; Large language model agent; Model agents; Model-based OPC; Payment protocol; Prompt injection; Red teaming; Decision making
ABSTRACT: Large language model (LLM)-based agents are increasingly used to automate financial transactions, but their reliance on contextual reasoning introduces new security risks. The Agent Payments Protocol (AP2) secures agent-mediated purchases through cryptographically signed mandates; however, its robustness against reasoning-layer attacks remains unclear. This paper presents a systematic red-teaming evaluation of AP2 and identifies vulnerabilities arising from prompt injection. Two attack techniques are introduced: the Branded Whisper Attack, which manipulates product ranking through adversarial content, and the Vault Whisper Attack, which induces cross-user data disclosure through crafted prompts. Using a functional AP2-based shopping agent implemented with Gemini-2.5-Flash and the Google Agent Development Kit (ADK), the evaluation demonstrates that indirect prompt injection achieves a 100% success rate in manipulating product ranking, while direct prompt injection leads to cross-account data exposure in 20% of cases. These attacks succeed without breaking cryptographic enforcement, operating entirely through reasoning-layer manipulation. The results reveal a fundamental limitation of agent-mediated payment systems: cryptographic guarantees ensure execution correctness but do not protect decision-making. Securing such systems requires additional mechanisms that constrain how contextual inputs influence agent reasoning. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0123] year=2026 | venue=IEEE International Conference on Automation, Quality and Testing, Robotics, AQTR | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Integrating Edge AI and Distributed IoT Architectures for Privacy-Preserving Smart Home Automation
