ABSTRACT: Converging IoT and Artificial Intelligence (AI) drives the transition to cognitive smart environments. However, current cloud-centric solutions introduce critical privacy, latency, and reliability vulnerabilities. This paper presents the design of a decentralized smart home system based on Edge Computing. The proposed architecture utilizes a Raspberry Pi 4 as a central Cognitive Hub, integrating a hybrid Generative AI agent (LLM). While local STT (Vosk) ensures initial audio processing to maintain voice privacy, the Gemini API is integrated to provide advanced semantic reasoning on the resulting text. This enables the system to move beyond rigid, keyword-based commands towards natural language understanding, where complex user intents are dynamically mapped to specific IoT control payloads. Unlike traditional rigid command systems, this agent semantically interprets natural language inputs and dynamically generates complex JSON control payloads for the distributed ESP32 microcontrollers via MQTT. A key contribution is the implementation of a real-time solar tracking and weather-adaptive control for energy optimization, which augments a local Proportional controller with external meteorological data to preemptively adjust system behavior during adverse weather. Furthermore, the system integrates local Artificial Intelligence (AI) for biometric security and context-aware safety protocols, enabling rapid, autonomous response to hazards like gas leaks without cloud reliance. Preliminary results demonstrate that this modular architecture offers a scalable, low-cost, and privacy-centric alternative to proprietary ecosystem. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0124] year=2026 | venue=Dianzi Yu Xinxi Xuebao/Journal of Electronics and Information Technology | type=Article
TITLE: AutoPenGPT: Drift-Resistant Penetration Testing Driven by Search-Space Convergence and Dependency Modeling
KEYWORDS: Industrial control system security; Intelligent penetration testing; Large language models; Web applications; Automation; Control systems; Distributed computer systems; Intelligent control; Knowledge based systems; Network security; Security systems; Capture the flag; Control system security; Industrial control system security; Industrial control systems; Intelligent penetration testing; Language model; Large language model; Penetration testing; WEB application; Web applications; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Objective Industrial Control Systems (ICS) are widely deployed in critical sectors and often contain longstanding vulnerabilities due to strict availability requirements and limited patching opportunities. The increasing exposure of external management and access infrastructure has expanded the attack surface and allows adversaries to pivot from boundary components into fragile production networks. Continuous penetration testing of these components is essential but remains costly and difficult to scale when carried out manually. Recent work examines Large Language Models (LLMs) for automated penetration testing; however, existing systems often experience strategy drift and intention drift, which produce incoherent testing behaviors and ineffective exploitation chains. Methods This study proposes AutoPenGPT, a multi-agent framework for automated Web security testing. AutoPenGPT uses an adaptive exploration-space convergence mechanism that predicts likely vulnerability types from target semantics and constrains LLM-driven testing through a dynamically updated payload knowledge base. To reduce intention drift in multi-step exploitation, a dependency-driven strategy module rewrites historical feedback, models step dependencies, and generates coherent, executable strategies in a closed-loop workflow. A semi-structured prompt embedding scheme is also developed to support heterogeneous penetration testing tasks while preserving semantic integrity. Results and Discussions AutoPenGPT is evaluated on Capture-the-Flag (CTF) benchmarks and real-world ICS and Web platforms. On CTF datasets, it achieves 97.62% vulnerability-type detection accuracy and an 80.95% requirement completion rate, exceeding state-of-the-art tools by a wide margin. In real-world deployments, it reaches approximately 70% requirement completion and identifies seven previously undisclosed vulnerabilities, demonstrating practical effectiveness. Conclusions The contributions are threefold. (1) Strategy drift and intention drift in LLM-driven penetration testing are examined and addressed through adaptive exploration and dependency-aware strategy mechanisms that stabilize long-horizon testing behaviors. (2) AutoPenGPT is designed and implemented as a multi-agent penetration testing system that integrates semantic vulnerability prediction, closed-loop strategy generation, and semi-structured prompt embedding. (3) Extensive evaluation on CTF and real-world ICS and Web platforms confirms the effectiveness and practicality of the system, including the discovery of previously unknown vulnerabilities. © (2026), (Science Press). All right reserved.

