ABSTRACT: Modern day urban security systems still face challenges in obtaining wide-area, real-time situational awareness-both of which are the results of limited camera coverage, fragmented sensor infrastructure and ultimately rely on human operators for threat perception and action. To address these limitations, we propose ARGUS, a multi-sensor aerial-ground surveillance infrastructure that integrates unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), unmanned ground vehicles (UGVs), and urban sensor networks to function as a coherent and self-sustained observation network. ARGUS incorporates YOLO-based anomaly detection, SLAM-based localization and mapping, and LLM-enabled contextual reasoning to sense events, understand scene semantics, and decide response actions. The control framework is based on ROS2, synchronized data fusion from cameras, IMU and GPS is supported for continuous scene understanding and cross-platform cooperation. A realistic urban simulation environment incorporating road obstacles and typical urban features to test the performance of the system. Experimental results show that ARGUS is able to detect road accidents in a reliable manner, extract accurate geographic coordinates, and autonomously send structured emergency alerts over a cloud-connected messaging pipeline. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0126] year=2025 | venue=Sensors | type=Article
TITLE: Large Language Models for Synthetic Dataset Generation of Cybersecurity Indicators of Compromise
