ABSTRACT: In the field of Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI), the scarcity of high-quality and labelled datasets that include Indicators of Compromise (IoCs) impact the design and implementation of robust predictive models that are capable of classifying IoCs in online communication, specifically in social media contexts where users are potentially highly exposed to cyber threats. Thus, the generation of high-quality synthetic datasets can be utilized to fill this gap and develop effective CTI systems. Therefore, this study aims to fine-tune OpenAI’s Large Language Model (LLM), Gpt-3.5, to generate a synthetic dataset that replicates the style of a real social media curated dataset, as well as incorporates select IoCs as domain knowledge. Four machine-learning (ML) and deep-learning (DL) models were evaluated on two generated datasets (one with 4000 instances and the other with 12,000). The results indicated that, on the 4000-instance dataset, the Dense Neural Network (DenseNN) outputs the highest accuracy (77%), while on the 12,000-instance dataset, Logistic Regression (LR) achieved the highest accuracy of 82%. This study highlights the potential of integrating fine-tuned LLMs with domain-specific knowledge to create high-quality synthetic data. The main contribution of this research is in the adoption of fine-tuning of an LLM, Gpt-3.5, using real social media datasets and curated IoC domain knowledge, which is expected to improve the process of synthetic dataset generation and later IoC extraction and classification, offering a realistic and novel resource for cybersecurity applications. © 2025 by the authors.

[R0127] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Big Data, BigData | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Towards Explainable and Educational Phishing Detection: A Zero-Shot LLM Approach
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Explainable AI (XAI); Human-Computer Interaction (HCI); Large Language Model (LLM); Phishing Website Detection; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Deep learning; HTTP; Human computer interaction; Learning systems; Natural language processing systems; Network security; Visual languages; Computer interaction; Cyber security; Detection system; Explainable AI (XAI); Human-computer interaction; Language model; Large language model; Phishing detections; Phishing website detection; Phishing websites; Websites
ABSTRACT: Phishing attacks have reached record levels, with attackers increasingly employing sophisticated evasion techniques such as cloaking, fingerprinting, and CAPTCHAs to circumvent traditional detection systems. Most existing approaches analyze webpage source code, JavaScript, and HTTP headers-artifacts that users never see and that can be manipulated to evade automated scanners. Moreover, deep learning-based detectors function as black boxes, producing accurate predictions but failing to provide interpretable explanations that build user resilience or support security analyst workflows. This disconnect between detection mechanisms and real-world incident reporting creates significant operational challenges, as users naturally capture screenshots and URLs when encountering suspicious pages through help-desk tickets or browser reporting inter-faces. We present a zero-shot explainable phishing detection system that operates exclusively on webpage screenshots and URLs-precisely the evidence users capture in practice. Our approach leverages GPT-4 Vision, a large multimodal language model, to achieve brand recognition and phishing intent detection without requiring task-specific training data. Through carefully designed chain-of-thought prompting, the system decomposes detection into interpretable reasoning steps: identifying the impersonated brand from visual and textual cues, analyzing credential request mechanisms, and detecting social engineering tactics. This structured reasoning process generates natural language explanations as integral components of detection rather than post-hoc rationalizations, making the system inherently explainable. We evaluate our system on the CIRCL Phishing Dataset. Our zero-shot approach achieves 82.0 percent accuracy on brand recognition. Additionally, our system provides an explainable AI agent that transforms technical outputs into multi-level educational explanations tailored to end users, security ana-lysts, and training programs, with visual annotations highlighting suspicious elements. Our work demonstrates that zero-shot large language models can achieve state-of-the-art phishing detection while providing inherent explainability, immediate deployment capability, and adaptability to emerging threats through prompt engineering. By aligning with real-world incident response work-flows and transforming detection from reactive blacklisting into proactive user education, this approach addresses critical gaps in both accuracy and interpretability for practical cybersecurity defense. © 2025 IEEE.

