[R0135] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE World AI IoT Congress, AIIoT 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SemGuard: A Hierarchical Shared Semantic Intelligence Architecture for Secure LLM Systems
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; AI Security; Input Sanitization; Large Language Model (LLM) Security; n8n; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Semantic Threat Detection; Vector Similarity Search; Architecture; Information retrieval; Knowledge based systems; Knowledge management; Natural language processing systems; Network security; Open source software; Open systems; Semantics; Agentic AI; AI security; Input sanitization; Language model; Large language model security; Model security; N8n; Retrieval-augmented generation; Sanitization; Semantic threat detection; Similarity search; Threat detection; Vector similarity; Vector similarity search; Search engines
ABSTRACT: Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in healthcare, finance, legal services, and enterprise knowledge management, where they process highly sensitive data. However, security controls have not kept pace with this adoption. Unlike traditional software, LLMs expose an attack surface defined by natural language, making general defenses such as safety tuning, keyword blocklists, and generic classifiers insufficient for deployment-specific threats. This limitation is especially critical because the risks faced by medical, financial, and other enterprise systems differ fundamentally by domain. This paper presents SemGuard, a hierarchical shared-semantic intelligence architecture for secure LLM deployments. The architecture distributes security across localized and domain-specialized filter nodes, each backed by a vectorized knowledge base of representative threat seeds, and augments input sanitization with semantic similarity search by repurposing retrieval-augmented generation infrastructure for real-time threat detection. A shared intelligence layer accumulates blocked prompts across nodes, enabling collective learning and cross-domain threat coverage without manual retraining. We implement and evaluate SemGuard using open-source components, including n8n, ChromaDB, and both locally hosted and cloud-hosted LLMs, demonstrating a practical and deployable approach to adaptive LLM security. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0136] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 5th International Conference on AI in Cybersecurity, ICAIC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Confidential and Attack-Resilient Edge LLM Serving for Multilingual Chatbots
KEYWORDS: confidential inference; edge computing; multilingual NLP; policy enforcement; remote attestation; security and privacy; Chatbots; Computer privacy; Cryptography; Data privacy; Network security; Proxy caches; Chatbots; Confidential inference; Edge computing; Language model; Multilingual NLP; Network edges; Policy enforcement; Privacy risks; Remote attestation; Security and privacy; Edge computing
ABSTRACT: Large Language Model (LLM) chatbots increasingly run at the network edge to reduce latency and cost, but edge deployments expand the attack surface and amplify privacy risk. We present Aegis-Edge, a confidential and attack-resilient serving stack for multilingual LLM inference on untrusted edge nodes. Aegis-Edge combines (i) remote attestation to verify the serving runtime before model and key release, (ii) confidential inference using trusted execution environments with a sealed key-value cache and tenant-scoped encryption, (iii) policy-as-code guardrails for locale-aware PII redaction and tool-use restrictions, and (iv) a lightweight adversarial input filter targeting multilingual prompt-injection and cache-poisoning attempts. We formalize security invariants over the request-response path and show how they compose across edge, gateway, and KMS. Our implementation supports common edge accelerators and multilingual tokenizers. We empirically evaluate security (attack success rate, leakage proxies, integrity violations), privacy (PII detection/retention bounds), and systems overhead (added latency, throughput, energy) under red-team workloads spanning diverse languages and scripts. Results demonstrate that robust privacy and integrity guarantees can be enforced at the edge while maintaining application-level service objectives. We discuss limitations (e.g., side-channels) and pathways for incremental deployment. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0137] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Intelligent Systems and Digital Applications, ISDA 2026 - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Zero-Trust Autonomy Cryptographically Enforced Safety and Policy Compliance in Clinical AI Agents
KEYWORDS: anomaly detection; cyber threat prevention; data privacy; patient data protection; virtual healthcare security; zero-trust framework; Artificial intelligence; Computer architecture; Health risks; Information leakage; Learning systems; Medical computing; Network security; Patient treatment; Privacy-preserving techniques; Virtual reality; Anomaly detection; Cybe threat prevention; Cyber threats; Mental health; Patient data; Patient data protection; Trust frameworks; User activity; Virtual healthcare security; Zero-trust framework; Anomaly detection
ABSTRACT: As virtual mental health adoption continues to grow, more sensitive patient information is becoming an easy target of cyber threats, such as unauthorized access, information leaks, and privacy breaches. The security and integrity of these online therapy sessions would be vital and especially so given that patients with chronic conditions such as diabetes, thyroid, and mental issues are conducting online sessions. The paper introduces a zero-trust AI-based architecture of cybersecurity system in virtual therapy to implement privacy and policy adherence and identify and prevent real-time breaches of privacy and unusual user activity. To do that, text-based communications are analyzed with the help of large language models (LLaMA 3.2 3B and Med42 v2 8B) to determine the possible data leak or breach of privacy. An encryption algorithm based on the block-wise concept with a lightweight is used in securing sensitive communications and deep reinforcement learning (DRL) is adopted in identifying anomalies in the user activity and access patterns to the system. A simulated environment of 25 synthetic patient sessions used to sample anonymized session logs, access records, communication logs and threat detection metrics and then preprocessed such that identifiable information was removed and forms uniformed before being used to train and test the models. The architecture had 94.7% accuracy in anomaly detection and 87% less exposure of data but preserved integrity of the session, imposed privacy laws, and allowed automated response, which proves the efficiency of AI and cryptographic protection against virtual mental health platforms. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0138] year=2026 | venue=Lecture Notes in Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Multi-agent Collaborative Framework with Few-Shot CoT for Threat Detection
KEYWORDS: Anomaly Detection; Chain-of-Thought; Cybersecurity; Few-Shot Learning; Multi-Agent Systems; Threat Detection; Anomaly detection; Chains; Deep learning; Distributed computer systems; Intelligent agents; Learning systems; Anomaly detection; Chain-of-thought; Collaborative framework; Cyber security; Data scarcity; Few-shot learning; Insider threat detections; Multi agent; Multiagent systems (MASs); Threat detection; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: To address the challenges of data scarcity, dynamic threats, and real-time detection in complex enterprise networks, this paper proposes a multi-agent collaborative framework integrated with Few-Shot Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning for insider threat detection. The framework coordinates specialized agents—Manager, Behavior Analyst, Searcher, and Reflector—to execute end-to-end log analysis, anomaly detection, and rule refinement. By combining Few-Shot Learning with explicit CoT reasoning, the system generates interpretable detection rules from limited samples and dynamically optimizes them through multi-agent feedback. Evaluations on the CERT-IT r4.2 dataset demonstrate that our method outperforms traditional machine learning, deep learning, and zero-shot/few-shot LLM baselines, achieving 8–12% higher accuracy and 6–23% improvement in F1-score across diverse attack scenarios. Notably, the framework attains 92% rule interpretability and converges to stable rules within 1–2 iterations, reducing false positives/negatives by 30–45% compared to static approaches. These results highlight the effectiveness of integrating multi-agent collaboration and Few-Shot CoT reasoning for scalable, adaptive, and explainable insider threat detection. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.

[R0139] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security | type=Conference paper
TITLE: PentestAgent: Incorporating LLM Agents to Automated Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Agent; Large Language Model; Penetration Testing; Automation; Benchmarking; Human computer interaction; Intelligent agents; Knowledge management; Multi agent systems; Security of data; Complex Processes; Critical technique; Entry point; Language model; Large language model; Penetration testing; Security vulnerabilities; Target systems; Testing knowledge; Testing method; Agents
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is a critical technique for identifying security vulnerabilities, traditionally performed manually by skilled security specialists. This complex process involves gathering information about the target system, identifying entry points, exploiting the system, and reporting findings. Despite its effectiveness, manual penetration testing is time-consuming and expensive, often requiring significant expertise and resources that many organizations cannot afford. While automated penetration testing methods have been proposed, they often fall short in real-world applications due to limitations in flexibility, adaptability, and implementation. Recent advancements in large language models offer new opportunities for enhancing penetration testing through increased intelligence and automation. However, current LLM-based approaches still face significant challenges, including limited penetration testing knowledge and a lack of comprehensive automation capabilities. To address these gaps, we propose PentestAgent, a novel LLM-based automated penetration testing framework that leverages the power of LLMs and various LLM-based techniques like retrieval augmented generation to enhance penetration testing knowledge and automate various tasks. Our framework leverages multi-agent collaboration to automate intelligence gathering, vulnerability analysis, and exploitation stages, reducing manual intervention. We evaluate PentestAgent using a comprehensive benchmark, demonstrating superior performance in task completion and overall efficiency. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

[R0140] year=2026 | venue=Computer Science Review | type=Article
TITLE: Beyond vulnerabilities: A comprehensive survey of adversarial attacks across domains
KEYWORDS: Adversarial attacks; Artificial intelligence; Black-box; Cybersecurity; Internet of things; Machine learning; White-box; Adversarial machine learning; Anomaly detection; Big data; Computational linguistics; Cybersecurity; Industrial management; Learning algorithms; Learning systems; Machine learning; Natural language processing systems; Network security; Speech recognition; Attack mechanism; Autonomous Vehicles; Black boxes; Cyber security; Defence mechanisms; Industrial control systems; Machine learning systems; Machine-learning; Natural languages; White box; Internet of things
ABSTRACT: Adversarial attacks present significant risks to machine learning (ML) systems, exploiting model vulnerabilities and threatening the integrity, security, and trustworthiness of applications across multiple sectors. This paper provides a comprehensive review of adversarial attack types—white box, black box, and other type of attacks—and examines tailored attacks and defense mechanisms across domains such as Internet of Things (IoT), healthcare, industrial control systems, autonomous vehicles, speech recognition, natural language processing (NLP), finance, and Large Language Models (LLMs). Each domain introduces unique adversarial challenges and demands specific countermeasures, from anomaly detection to adversarial training and robust model architectures. By systematically categorizing both attack methodologies and defense strategies, this survey offers a holistic understanding of adversarial dynamics across fields, highlighting critical areas for further research and the development of resilient, cross-domain ML defenses. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Inc.


